  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nIssued 248 PM MST THU NOV 12 2026\nThe next low pressure system will be moving into the area\nstarting Sunday. On Sunday, precipitation chances will be \nlimited to eastern Oregon before spreading into SW Idaho by \nSunday evening. The trough will hold together enough for a \n50-70% chance of light precipitation late Sunday through \nTuesday. A cold front will slowly move through the area west-to-\neast, starting early Monday. Breezy prefrontal southwesterly \nsurface winds will warm temperatures by a few degrees Sunday \ncompared to Saturday, switching to cooler northwesterly flow \nMonday and Tuesday. Snow levels will fall from 5500-6500 feet \nSunday night to 4000-6000 feet by Tuesday, lowest across eastern\nOregon. A brief dry period is expected early Wednesday morning \nbefore the next system moves in. This deep upper-level low will \nmove overhead from mid to late week, bringing colder air than \nprevious systems. Uncertainty currently surrounds just how cool \nthe temperature will get and the extent of the accompanying \nprecipitation. Regardless, precipitation chances are currently \nforecast at 30-50% late Wednesday through Thursday, with \ntemperatures favored to cool a few more degrees and snow levels \naround 3500-5000 feet.",
